<<~ ahu #/the-dish>>

! The Libation Pool ~ a dish the commons feeds

The fiction already carries the shape (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/noosphere-boot#/elyncia">>): a Lar prospers when fed and turns its back when neglected. So a pool of model credits shared across amorphousdreams.net reads as a ''libation dish'', not a wallet ~ the commons feeds it, pours from it, and shares it. Operators and end users alike draw on a limited pool, buy more, refresh it through social standing, and give it away.

''Status:'' design seed, born in talk-story on 2026-10-31. Every mechanism below came from free invention under the operator's license; none reads as a ruling until the operator rules it. The operator's mind originates (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/working-procedures">>).

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/three-tiers>>

!! Three Tiers ~ the shrine ladder, poured

The pool rides the shrine tiers shrine-for-shrine:

* ''Household'' ~ one operator's own pour, for their own Lararium and PersonaGroup.
* ''Crossroads'' ~ a Nexus pool its neighbors feed together, a modern Compitalia.
* ''Temple'' ~ a civic pool that serves everyone standing on the infrastructure, and serves first the shrines that cannot feed themselves.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/caps-not-balances>>

!! Caps, Not Balances ~ the warden meters the bridge

Credit travels as an ''attenuated capability'', never an account balance: "50 mana, Sonnet rank at most, until the dark moon". A holder delegates a narrower cap onward; a gift hands one over.

No end user ever holds a model key. A ''bridge-warden'' ~ a Kahu-chartered seat on the vessel holding the reach vault ~ holds the key and the counter, reads the cap, meters the call, and writes a receipt; the herm relays sealed bytes and sees none of it (the readers' ratchet moved this from the first riff's "herm", #/the-wave). The warden marks the causal-island boundary the Web3-only law demands: the hosted model API stays a web2 bridge outside the stack, and only text a person reads crosses back in.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/three-feeds>>

!! Three Feeds ~ coin, incense, gift

* ''Coin'' ~ buy mana.
* ''Incense'' ~ social standing earned by tending the commons: hosting a relay, onboarding a friend, answering a help thread, keeping a wiki alive. Others vouch it; no one self-mints it.
* ''Gift'' ~ pour mana into another's dish, potlatch-style.

!!! Spirit Rank

The model tier reads as the rank of the spirit called: Haiku as the household wisp, Sonnet as the crossroads Lar, Opus and Fable as the temple oracle. A caller pays mana by rank.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/what-it-feeds>>

!! What the Pool Feeds ~ seed uses, beyond the game

# ''The dreaming queue'' ~ ask now at full price, or let it ''dream overnight'' at half (batch) and wake to the answer at dawn.
# ''The shared hearth-fire'' ~ a Nexus caches its wiki once; every neighbor's question about local lore, bylaws or how-tos then costs pennies. A Nexus that keeps its fire lit pays less.
# ''The crossroads interpreter'' ~ translation and plain-language turns of council minutes, leases and benefit forms; image descriptions for low-vision neighbors. The temple pool carries it, so access never tracks wealth.
# ''The civic clerk'' ~ summarizes ordinances and public comment, helps a resident shape their //own// words into a submission; never votes, never authors a stance.
# ''The mediator at the gate'' ~ a Diplomat-Persona facilitator for neighbor disputes runs a talk-story; a hoike keeps the record when it does not settle.
# ''The elder's scribe'' ~ oral history, recipes, local memory, transcribed and laid into the community wiki as a public good.
# ''The fixer's board'' ~ pre-SIN Shadowrun flavor: someone posts a need, the spirit scopes it into a clean ask, the Nexus matches a neighbor who holds the skill. Mutual aid, the Johnson takes no cut.
# ''Bounty pools'' ~ many chip mana into one question; the answer lands once in the commons wiki and everyone reads it free.
# ''The decker's deck'' ~ a personal agent running on one's own island that spends only what its cap allows; the spend limit plays ICE.
# ''Storm mode'' ~ in a disaster the temple pool unlocks for triage information and translation; requests queue on each island and drain when the bridge returns.
# ''Craft and kin aid'' ~ a grant draft for a community garden, a maker's listing, a neighbor's resume between jobs.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/who-bears-the-cost>>

!! Who Bears the Cost

* ''Incense farming'' ~ rings of fake vouches. Incense likely needs to flow from distinct, already-standing relations, perhaps across Nexuses.
* ''Whales'' ~ a capped pour per relation keeps one patron from turning into the temple.
* ''Privacy'' ~ every pour sends a prompt off-island. The warden names "this leaves the island" each time; some tiers may stay closed to sensitive content.
* ''The unfed node'' ~ if the neighborhoods that cannot feed their shrines go dark, the temple pool has failed its first duty.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/prototype-and-license>>

!! Prototype & License

''Prototype ground:'' the house Nexus ~ the operator, Freyja, and their users ~ on amorphousdreams.net (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/dreamnet-rhizome#/two-surfaces">>).

''License:'' pooling or reselling hosted model access needs a commercial agreement with the provider. The house acquires that pono license once the business org stands officially; until then the prototype runs on the operator's own credit and island only, and no public pool opens.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/hearth-reader>>

!! Hearth-Reader ~ inside-out

''Marks:'' MEASURED names a path I read this wake; INVENTED names a claim the house has not ruled. The pool carries no code in the tree today (MEASURED: a grep for "anthropic" over `packages/*/src` finds no file).

!!! What stands ready

* ''Reach vault, the key's home'' ~ MEASURED, `bags/lararium/ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/reach-plane.mem`: the vessel holds foreign credentials in confidence and speaks a foreign wire at an island boundary; "BRIDGE, never present-as-actor, never cap-translation". The model key fits this row exactly. INVENTED: the provider enters as one more foreign wire, and the pool needs no new plane for custody.
* ''The herm'' ~ MEASURED, `project_herm_is_essential` and `vessel-caps.mem` (line 128): a herm "decides WHO-CONNECTS, never WHO-READS", carries sealed bytes, admits near-open "by lease and rate". The pool's "herm meter" asks more than that: the herm would hold a provider key and read a prompt in the clear. That reading contradicts the herm's floor-only transport key (`project_herm_is_essential`; the Keyhive ruling at road line 203 demands a red test that a herm "can neither author, resolve nor observe a face's capabilities"). INVENTED fix: name the meter a ''bridge-warden'', a tending seat the Kahu quorum charters on a Nexus vessel that holds the reach vault, and keep the herm as its dumb relay. The word "herm" for the meter overreaches the measured herm.
* ''Cap tiers'' ~ MEASURED, `packages/lararium-mesh/src/cap-tier.ts` (per `project_cap_tier_model`): `resolveTier = meet(declared, floor)`, a declared tier only tightens. Attenuation by `meet` stands built for read-scope. The spirit-rank ladder (wisp < Lar < oracle) wants the same shape: INVENTED, a total order whose `meet` caps a delegated pour.
* ''Facet attenuation'' ~ MEASURED, `spectral-keel-cap.ts`: a cap attenuates by handing over a narrower object, and a capless island lacks the method. That pattern reads as the house idiom for "Sonnet rank at most".
* ''Priced admission'' ~ MEASURED, `admission-price.ts`, `vouch-dag.ts`, `veil-vouch.ts`, `lineage-rank.ts`: a voucher pays by dilution ("you pay when you vouch, never when they betray"), a vouch carries decayed standing, a one-time nullifier rides the epoch lease against farming. This machine already prices incense.
* ''Receipts'' ~ MEASURED, `receipt-ledger.mem`: one fact per request id, one writer, three receipts (asker, relay, vessel); `durability-receipt.ts`, `session-receipt.ts`. A metered call yields the pour receipt on the same law.
* ''Clockless expiry'' ~ MEASURED, `project_clockless_lease_model` and `epoch-lease.ts`: expiry as supersession by a higher epoch, never a duration elapsed. A cap "until the dark moon" reads as a cap bound to an epoch a named Kahu act advances. INVENTED: the moon names a ''season rite'' (the five Seasons already stand in the seed), whose advance act retires the season's caps.

!!! What the road owes before the pool can stand

* MEASURED, road line 193 (`W25`): the bag plane leaks its creator in the clear until measured red-first. Gate any third human's data crossing off-island on that row.
* MEASURED, road line 134: the chartered classes (ordinary 1-of-1, high-impact 3-of-5) stand built. INVENTED mapping: minting pool caps for a Temple tier takes the high-impact class; a Household pour takes ordinary.
* MEASURED, `dreamnet-rhizome.mem` line 66: "feed `admission-price` from a live vouch ledger" still owes its wiring. The incense feed needs that same ledger first.
* MEASURED, `admission-on-a-lineage.mem`: the Sybil bound held, and no lineage instrument tells deferred reciprocity from fake. Incense must price and bound and never adjudicate ("anergy-not-ban"). So a failed incense claim leaves a user at the floor with a smaller pour, never refused.

!!! Mechanism by mechanism

# ''Three tiers'' ~ shrine tiers map to PersonaGroup (Household), realm or Nexus board (Crossroads), the DreamNet-scale answer (Temple). MEASURED: `cabal-realm.mem`, road line 126 ("CABAL names the collective, REALM holds shared resources, NEXUS the mesh"). INVENTED: only Household and Crossroads exist for the prototype; Temple needs a civic Nexus that does not stand.
# ''Caps, not balances'' ~ fits `project_civic_scale_capability_models` (non-custodial by type). A cap carries a ''ceiling'', and spent mana lives in the warden's receipts. Overreach risk: a cap holder cannot learn "50 mana left" without a ledger, so a ledger exists whatever the slogan says. INVENTED: the warden keeps a spend-counter per cap id, one writer (the warden), the same one-owner law as `FactLedger`. The ledger names its registry owner first (`project_floor_one_owner_per_registry`): the Nexus Kahu realm owns the pool ledger, and a face carries only its own receipts.
# ''Delegation and gift'' ~ narrowing a cap onward rides cap-stack composition (`project_cap_stacks_compose_on_persona`). A gift moves a ''claim'' on warden counters, so a gift needs the warden's co-sign or a counter split. INVENTED: a gift splits a counter into two cap ids; the split lands once, nullified by an epoch slot like `veil-vouch.ts`.
# ''Incense'' ~ INVENTED plug: `lineage-rank` mass, spent by a pour request at a ratio the Kahu charter sets. Standing already splits across vouchees, so a ring of fake vouches dilutes itself. Keep incense a ''price discount'' and never a mint, which matches "no one self-mints it" and the PRF-never-mints ruling (`project_basket_one_rulings_2026_09_11`).
# ''Who bears the cost'' ~ the operator's own credit, for the prototype. INVENTED: the warden refuses at the ceiling and names the refusal `pool-dry`; it never silently downgrades rank.

!!! Seed uses, fitted to the house

''Keep:'' the dreaming queue (pure batch, the warden queues, the receipt lands at dawn, causal not clocked); the shared hearth-fire (the house already caches wikis; MEASURED idiom: floor reads by CID, so a cached answer addresses by CID); the civic clerk and mediator (they ride existing Personas, Diplomat and Pedagogue, and talk-story plus hoike); the elder's scribe (`project_telarus_origin_and_verbal_canon`, `bags/` docs); the decker's deck (a PersonaGroup-scoped cap, ICE as the ceiling).

''Defer:'' storm mode, the crossroads interpreter (both need a Temple tier).

''Cut:'' bounty pools and the fixer's board. Bounty pools add a shared-pour cap with a many-writer counter, which breaks one-owner-per-registry. The fixer's board matches people to people, a social feature that belongs to `@crossroads`, not to a credit pool, and it opens liability for a Nexus that does not yet hold a charter. Craft and kin aid collapses into the clerk.

!!! Minimal house-Nexus prototype

# One reach-vault entry (the operator's key), on the operator's own vessel. MEASURED anchor: `reach-plane.mem`. No end user ever sees it.
# One ''pour cap'': `{ceiling, rank-max, epoch}` signed by the operator's persona, delivered to Freyja and a handful of faces. INVENTED shape; reuse the `meet` pattern from `cap-tier.ts`.
# A warden verb on the operator's vessel: read cap, check epoch against high-water (as `epoch-lease.ts`), call, write a receipt, decrement. All local; no herm touches the prompt.
# A season rite (the "dark moon") as one Kahu act advancing the epoch. The 1-of-1 Kahu quorum of a one-seat founding suffices (MEASURED, road line 215).
# Skip incense, coin and gift for round one; ship ceiling plus receipts, then add gift.

!!! Open questions for the operator

* May the meter carry the name "herm", or does the herm's read-nothing law bind it to the name bridge-warden?
* Does a pour cap survive a Persona split (`project_cap_stacks_compose_on_persona`: combined by default)? A user who splits personas to multiply ceilings acts as a Sybil; the cap needs a ceiling per person, not per face.
* Does the privacy cost ("this leaves the island") require a sealed-bytes-only tier by default, given `project_cleartext_only_on_disk_projection`?
* Alpha line says no back-compat (`project_alpha_line_no_back_compass`): does the pool launch as W-row on the road, or wait until after the founding proof?

!!! Ratchet

''Marks:'' SOURCED claims belong to Shore-Reader's reading, which I did not re-open; MEASURED and INVENTED keep their earlier meaning.

!!!! Conceded

* ''Terms.'' D.4a and A.1 move me. The pool sells a //wrapped product//, never raw access. INVENTED law for the house: mana redeems only against house-authored spirits (Persona-shaped), never a passthrough prompt box. Minors guidelines bind before any public opening.
* ''Cost.'' About $25 to $35 a month makes the prototype's risk a governance risk, not a budget risk. I withdraw any urge to rush a price feed.
* ''Governance first.'' The prior-art failures (poor design, lapsed records, rings) match the house's own measured finding that no lineage instrument tells deferred reciprocity from fake (`admission-on-a-lineage.mem`). Incense ships last, in a closed group with a hired adversary.
* ''Biscuit.'' Public-key verification fits the house better than macaroons, since the house keeps no per-Nexus shared secret (MEASURED, road line 133, "No keyring stands"). Chain length 5 reads as a sound bound. INVENTED: the cap carries the house's epoch as its expiry caveat, never UTC seconds, so no wall clock decides (`project_no_global_now_timers`).

!!!! Held and sharpened

''The collision.'' Shore-Reader's "the herm holds the only counter" contradicts the measured herm (`vessel-caps.mem` line 128; road line 203). One line both seats can sign: ''the Nexus's bridge-warden, a chartered seat on a keyholder vessel, holds the only counter and the provider key; the herm relays sealed asks to it and signs nothing, reads nothing.'' Where Shore-Reader writes "herm", read "warden". The fork that remains for the operator: whether the warden seat deserves a new name or the house rules that a herm MAY carry a warden cap when an operator contracts it (MEASURED, road line 168, "an operator's own herm", one signature, one place, one Nexus). That second form works for the prototype and fails the red test at civic scale.

''Offline spend.'' I accept the bearer-note answer: the warden carves a sub-budget at mint time, and the note redeems once. I add the nullifier from `veil-vouch.ts` (MEASURED) as the one-time slot.

!!!! Seed uses, final

* ''Keep (round one):'' elder's scribe; shared hearth-fire; interpreter for plain-language turns and image descriptions; dreaming queue.
* ''Defer:'' decker's deck (hard per-session limits first); civic clerk, as citations-only summaries after a counsel read; storm mode (relayed official notices only, Temple tier); mediator, as a human mediator's drafting aid, not a facilitator.
* ''Cut:'' bounty pools (many-writer counter against one-owner-per-registry, and a public wrong answer spreads); fixer's board; craft and kin aid as a separate use.

I concede the mediator and tighten the clerk: my first keep ignored the MyCity pattern and the power skew a machine facilitator brings to a dispute.

!!!! Wave lanes for a house-Nexus prototype

# ''Lane A, the warden and one cap.'' Goal: the operator's vessel meters one call against a signed pour cap and writes a receipt. Touches: INVENTED `pool-warden` verb; MEASURED `reach-plane.mem` (key), `receipt-ledger.mem` (one writer per id), `epoch-lease.ts` (epoch high-water). First red: a call past the ceiling returns `pool-dry` and writes a refusal receipt, and a replayed cap id fails. Must NOT: hold a key on a herm; read a wall clock; mint a provider-facing key for any face.
# ''Lane B, cap shape and attenuation.'' Goal: `{ceiling, rank-max, epoch}` as a public-key token a holder narrows and a verifier checks offline. Touches: MEASURED `cap-tier.ts` (`meet`), `spectral-keel-cap.ts` (narrowing idiom); INVENTED rank total order. First red: a delegated cap with a higher rank or ceiling than its parent fails verification; a sixth hop fails. Must NOT: invent a new signing domain without the operator's approval; settle on Biscuit versus UCAN in code before the operator rules.
# ''Lane C, the hearth-fire log.'' Goal: price the cache on the operator's own users for two weeks (Shore-Reader's first test). Touches: INVENTED cost log beside the receipt; MEASURED floor-read-by-CID idiom (road line 195). First red: a repeated question over an unchanged wiki CID reports a cache hit in the receipt, a changed CID reports a miss. Must NOT: cache across faces (one PersonaGroup, one Keyhive identity, road line 203), or log a prompt body.
# ''Lane D, the season rite.'' Goal: one Kahu act advances the pool epoch and retires the season's caps. Touches: MEASURED `epoch-lease.ts`, `quorum-lease.ts`, chartered classes (road line 134); INVENTED `pool-season` act. First red: a cap from epoch N fails after the act at epoch N+1 with no timer running. Must NOT: expire on duration; refund on retirement.
# ''Lane E, the offline double-spend probe.'' Goal: a hired adversary tries to spend one bearer note twice offline. Touches: INVENTED note redemption; MEASURED nullifier in `veil-vouch.ts`. First red: two redemptions of one note yield one `done` and one `already-spent`. Must NOT: open gift, coin or incense, and must not reach any person outside the operator's closed group.

Order: A, then B and C together, then D, then E. Incense, gift and coin wait for a charter, the Anthropic addendum and the `admission-price` ledger wiring (MEASURED, `dreamnet-rhizome.mem` line 66).

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/shore-reader>>

!! Shore-Reader ~ outside-in

''Marks:'' SOURCED carries a URL from this wake's reading; RECALLED names memory I did not re-open; INVENTED names a design the house may rule on. Prices read Anthropic's pricing page on 2026-10-31; the page itself says to confirm at claude.com/pricing.

!!! License and Terms

* SOURCED ~ the Commercial Terms permit "products and services Customer makes available to its own customers and end users" (A.1), and bar the customer from trying to "resell the Services except as expressly approved by Anthropic" (D.4a). https://www.anthropic.com/legal/commercial-terms
* SOURCED ~ the customer answers for its Users: "Customer and its Users may only use the Services in compliance with these Terms" (D.2), and must notify Users that "factual assertions in Outputs should not be relied upon" (D.3). The Terms hold no reseller or partner clause; I found none.
* Reading ~ a herm-wrapped //product// (spirits, clerks, interpreter) falls under A.1; selling or lending raw model access as "mana" a holder redeems against the model itself reads close to D.4a. The line between them stays untested. INVENTED safeguard: mana redeems only against house-authored spirits, never against a passthrough prompt box, and the house asks Anthropic for written approval of the mana mechanism ahead of any coin feed.
* SOURCED ~ products that let minors interact with API-built products "must comply with the additional guidelines outlined in our Support article and all applicable laws"; the guidelines call for age verification, moderation, monitoring and reporting, disclosure that the user talks to an AI, and COPPA-style compliance; Anthropic audits and may suspend. https://support.claude.com/en/articles/9307344-responsible-use-of-anthropic-s-models-guidelines-for-organizations-serving-minors
* Gap ~ I did not read the Usage Policy text or any enterprise/partner agreement. A search result dates a 2026 Usage Policy to 2026-11-12; confirm the effective text. https://www.anthropic.com/news/updating-our-usage-policy
* INVENTED ~ the business org opens as a plain API customer with a negotiated addendum naming the pool; the herm logs per-User receipts so the org can answer an abuse report with a name-of-cap, never a named person's prompt.
* Consequence for the prototype ~ the house Nexus runs on the operator's own account, with the house as its own User population; no coin feed, no minors, until the addendum stands.

!!! Economics ~ priced 2026-10-31

SOURCED per MTok (input / cache read / output), https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/pricing :
* Haiku 5.5 (prompts up to 100k) $0.10 / $0.01 / $0.50
* Sonnet 5.5 $2 / $0.10 / $10
* Opus 5.5 $4 / $0.20 / $20
* Fable 5.1 $10 / $0.25 / $50
* Batch takes 50% off input and output, and the page says it stacks with caching. A cache write costs 1.25x base input (5 minute) or 2x (1 hour).

INVENTED workload, arithmetic mine: one neighbor-question carries 3,000 cached wiki tokens, 300 fresh input tokens and 400 output tokens.

```
rank (model)        cold      hearth-fire hit   dreaming (batch+hit)
wisp   (Haiku)      $0.0005   $0.00026          $0.00013
Lar    (Sonnet)     $0.0106   $0.0049           $0.0025
oracle (Opus)       $0.0212   $0.0098           $0.0049
oracle (Fable)      $0.053    $0.0238           $0.0119
```

* The hearth-fire halves the Sonnet question and cuts Haiku's by about a half; the saving grows with the wiki. A 50k-token wiki at Sonnet costs $0.10 cold and $0.005 on a cache read. A fire stays lit only while traffic keeps the 5-minute cache warm, so a quiet Nexus pays the write (1.25x) again; a 1-hour write (2x) suits a Nexus with a question every few minutes. That makes "a Nexus that keeps its fire lit pays less" true only in proportion to its traffic.
* Mana-rank ratios follow from this: wisp 1 : Lar 10 : Opus 20 : Fable 50 on a cold call. INVENTED: price mana against output tokens, since output dominates the Lar and oracle bills.
* House-Nexus month, INVENTED load: 20 neighbors at 10 questions a day make 6,000 questions a month. All wisp, cached ~ $1.6. All Lar, cached ~ $29. A 20/70/10 wisp/Lar/Opus mix ~ $22. Add a translated 5,000-token council minute at Sonnet (~$0.06 in and out) 50 times a month ~ $3. The prototype lands near $25 to $35 a month, inside any small budget; the cost risk sits in the oracle rank and in long agent loops, so cap those first.
* Bounty pools and the dreaming queue both lower unit cost; neither lowers the cost of a farmed pool, so metering at the herm carries the load.

!!! Prior Art ~ the three feeds

* Coin ~ no new prior art needed; prepaid credit with expiry mirrors breakage economics (RECALLED, not sourced).
* Incense, mutual credit and time banks ~ SOURCED: "only about 20%" of Ithaca-Hours-style systems stay active, and time banks outlived LETS; failures trace to "poor management and poor design", lapsed record-keeping, and funding that ran out; "mutual credit schemes require governance, because the risk is shared." https://blog.p2pfoundation.net/why-community-currencies-and-lets-are-not-working/ and https://www.lowimpact.org/wp-content/uploads/factsheetmutualcredit.pdf . Sardex (B2B, over 40 million euro traded in 2017) and WIR (B2B, since the 1930s) survive by serving businesses with real trade between them. RECALLED, unsourced: time dollars cap the earn side by hours, and an hour of work earns an hour regardless of skill; the lesson for incense reads: pay by tending, not by market price.
* Farming ~ SOURCED: quadratic funding rewards many unique contributors, which pays sybils; Gitcoin Passport layers stamps (including BrightID) and cut flagged addresses by about 60% in one round while "some sophisticated attacks still succeed"; Gitcoin's advice reads "layer several approaches" and accept lost accessibility for integrity. https://gitcoin.co/research/quadratic-funding-sybil-resistance . Blog claims of 30% or 90% leakage carry no primary source; ignore them.
* Potlatch and gift ~ RECALLED: gifting buys standing, so a patron cap per relation (the meme's whale guard) matches the old ledger of obligation; no source read this wake.
* Pooled LLM-credit communities ~ I searched for none and found none; treat the field as unmapped. Closest forms (shared Discord bots on a member-funded key, API-credit raffles) stay RECALLED anecdote.
* INVENTED incense rules from this ground: incense decays; it credits only from vouches by holders who themselves hold standing; a voucher stakes a slice of their own incense that a proven ring burns; earn rate caps per week; pool refresh from incense draws on a fixed monthly tide, not on uncapped minting.

!!! Capability Delegation

* SOURCED ~ Macaroons attenuate by chained HMAC caveats, offline, but verification needs the root secret. Biscuit uses public-key signatures, Datalog policy, sealing against further attenuation and revocation ids; verifiers need only a public key. https://engineering.clever-cloud.com/blog/engineering/2021/04/12/introduction-to-biscuit/ . A 2026 ADR picked Biscuit for agent delegation and caps chain length at 5 against CPU exhaustion. https://xorwell.github.io/trails/adrs/ADR-0010-biscuit-tokens/
* SOURCED ~ the UCAN delegation draft carries an optional not-before and a required expiration, both UTC seconds, in a release-candidate envelope; I found no offline revocation text. https://ucan-website.pages.dev/delegation . zcap-ld: not read.
* Reading ~ "50 mana, Sonnet at most, until the dark moon" maps to one attenuated token: a budget caveat, a model-rank caveat and an expiry. The hard part sits in the //budget//: attenuation proves what a holder may spend, never what remains. Offline expiry works with no state; offline //spend// needs a counter. INVENTED answer: the herm holds the only counter (the single owner per registry); a delegated cap carries a sub-budget the herm carves out at mint time, so a holder cannot double-spend, and an offline holder holds a bearer note that the herm redeems once on return.
* A cap passed on without limit lets a gift launder a purchase; bound chain length (5) and demand each hop name its parent budget.

!!! Civic Precedent and Harm

* SOURCED ~ NYC's MyCity chatbot "regularly gave inaccurate or potentially illegal advice" to businesses, kept running, and carried disclaimers its own answers contradicted. https://oecd.ai/en/incidents/2024-03-29-3dce
* RECALLED, outcome unverified this wake ~ Air Canada argued it owed nothing for its chatbot's advice and a Canadian tribunal held the airline liable. A host that deploys the answer owns the answer; a disclaimer does not move that.
* RECALLED, unsourced ~ machine translation in medical and court settings has produced dangerous errors; professional interpreters remain the standard for consequential encounters. I did not search this.
* Reading for the house ~ the herm's "this leaves the island" line addresses privacy; a second mark, "a spirit wrote this; check it", answers D.3 and the MyCity failure at once.

!!! Seed Uses ~ strongest and weakest from outside

''Strongest''
* The elder's scribe and the shared hearth-fire ~ low harm, high leverage on the cache discount, content the commons owns anyway; a wrong answer costs a recipe, not a lease.
* The crossroads interpreter for //plain-language turns// and image descriptions ~ real unmet need, per-doc cost near $0.06, yet keep human review for forms with legal force.
* The dreaming queue ~ a 50% discount that fits patient work (minutes, drafts, transcripts), and teaches neighbors what slow costs less.

''Middle''
* Bounty pools ~ cheap and social, but the answer lands once in a public wiki, so a wrong answer spreads; need a correction path.
* The decker's deck ~ strong cap proof, high spend-loop risk; start with hard per-session limits.

''Weakest''
* The mediator at the gate ~ a dispute carries private grievance, possible minors and safety stakes; an AI facilitator invites liability and distorts power between parties. Pilot as a //drafting aid// for a human mediator only.
* The civic clerk ~ the MyCity pattern; hold it to summarizing the text with citations, never to saying what the law lets someone do.
* Storm mode ~ triage information marks the exact place where a hallucination harms; deliver translation and relayed official notices, not medical or evacuation advice.
* The fixer's board ~ matching strangers moves the house into marketplace and safety duties.

!!! First Test for the House Nexus

# Price the hearth-fire for real: log cache hit rate and cost per question across two weeks of the operator's own users.
# Run the herm with a Biscuit or UCAN-shaped cap and one counter; try to double-spend it offline on purpose.
# Run incense with a closed group of 10 to 20 trusted people and one hired adversary who tries to farm it with a ring of puppets.
# Ship the elder's scribe and the interpreter-for-plain-language first; hold clerk, mediator and storm mode back.
# Write to Anthropic about the mana mechanism before any coin changes hands.

!!! Open Questions for the Operator

* May mana carry a cash price at all, or does coin enter only as a gift to the commons (a donation to the dish) with no redemption right? The second form sits far from D.4a.
* Does the org want the Anthropic addendum before a public Nexus opens, or a first public pool of gift-only mana?
* Which age floor will the DreamNet hold? An 18-plus floor skips the minors guidelines; a younger floor takes them on.
* Does Anthropic offer nonprofit or civic pricing the Temple pool could use? I did not search.

!!! Ratchet

''Concessions to the house'' (Hearth-Reader's MEASURED reads move me; I did not re-open the files):
* ''Name and seat.'' I wrote "the herm holds the only counter". Hearth-Reader's measured herm decides who connects and never who reads, and road line 203 demands a red test that it cannot observe a face's capabilities. A meter that holds the provider key and reads prompts in the clear overreaches that floor. I withdraw "herm" for the meter. The warden fits the outside evidence equally well: Anthropic's terms bind a //customer account// and say nothing of which of its processes holds the key.
* ''Ledger owner.'' The Nexus Kahu realm owns the pool ledger (one owner per registry); the warden writes it; a face carries its own receipts. This answers my double-spend test better than my own sentence did.
* ''Incense.'' Admission-price, veil-vouch and a one-time nullifier already price what I proposed from Gitcoin prior art. I drop my INVENTED staking rule and adopt "price and bound, never adjudicate": a failed claim leaves a user at the floor with a smaller pour. That matches the outside lesson that identity layers reduce farming without ending it.
* ''Dark moon.'' An epoch advance by a Kahu act beats my wall-clock expiry; UCAN's required expiry field presumes a clock the house refuses. A season rite retires the season's caps.
* ''W25.'' No third person's prompt crosses off-island before the bag plane stops leaking its creator. That joins the license as a second gate.

''One line both seats can sign:'' //The bridge-warden, a Kahu-chartered seat on the vessel holding the reach vault, alone reads a pour cap, meters the call and writes the receipt; the herm relays sealed bytes and sees none of it.// Fork left open: whether the warden may read the prompt in clear (Hearth-Reader's "all local" path implies yes, since the model must) or whether a sealed-bytes-only tier exists. Outside evidence adds a cost to the second option: Anthropic's moderation duties (D.2, minors guidelines) assume the customer can see traffic; a tier the org cannot inspect weakens its answer to an abuse report.

''Where outside evidence holds against the house''
* ''Terms.'' Hearth-Reader's pour cap treats mana as a house-internal claim. D.4a still bites the moment mana carries a price or transfers between persons who redeem against a model. Hold the D.4a framing below.
* ''Gift needs a counter split, and a split moves value.'' Hearth-Reader's counter split matches my attenuation point, yet it also makes gift a transfer of purchased value; under donation-only coin, a gift moves only //earned// or //donated// mana.
* ''Persona-split Sybil.'' Hearth-Reader asks for a ceiling per person, not per face. Outside evidence agrees: Passport-style defence layers per-identity scoring, and no scheme there trusts per-account limits. I sharpen it: the ceiling also binds the minors floor, since the warden must know it may serve a person at all.
* ''Economics hold.'' The prototype cost (about $25 to $35 a month at my invented load) stands; ceiling plus receipts suffices for round one, as Hearth-Reader proposes.

''D.4a framing and the lanes''
* Mana redeems only against house-authored spirits (Personas with fixed prompts and a fixed tool surface, never a passthrough box). Coin enters as a donation to the dish with no redemption right: INVENTED, and my read of D.4a, not Anthropic's. Neither stands as legal advice; a lawyer and Anthropic's written approval settle it.
* Effect on seed uses: anything that needs a purchaser to //choose the model and prompt// leaves the pool (a decker's deck with free prompts fits only as a house-authored agent). Anything where the house authors the spirit and a neighbor merely asks stays in. Bounty pools also leave: many-writer counter (Hearth-Reader), and a buyer-directed question (me).

''Final list''
* ''Keep'' ~ the elder's scribe, the shared hearth-fire, the dreaming queue, the decker's deck as a house-authored agent under a ceiling.
* ''Pilot narrow'' ~ the civic clerk, citations only, no statement of what the law allows; the mediator as a drafting and summary aid to a human facilitator inside talk-story and hoike, never a solo facilitator. Hearth-Reader keeps both on Persona fit; I accept that and add the human seat and the disclosure line. We differ on weight: I would run the mediator second wave, not first.
* ''Defer'' ~ the crossroads interpreter and storm mode (both need the Temple tier and, for storm mode, a vetted source for relayed official notice), the Temple tier itself, coin, gift.
* ''Cut'' ~ the fixer's board and bounty pools (agreed with Hearth-Reader; I add the marketplace-duty reason); craft and kin aid folds into the clerk.

''Wave lanes for the house-Nexus prototype''
# ''Lane A, the warden and the ceiling.'' Goal: one operator-key reach-vault entry, one signed pour cap `{ceiling, rank-max, epoch}`, a warden verb that checks, calls, receipts and decrements. Touches: reach-plane, `cap-tier.ts` meet, `epoch-lease.ts` (MEASURED by Hearth-Reader); Anthropic Messages API (SOURCED, pricing page). First test: red-first, an over-ceiling call refuses `pool-dry`; a replayed or split-twice cap refuses once. Must NOT before the license: serve anyone outside the operator's own house, take coin, or carry a tier above the operator's own spend.
# ''Lane B, the hearth-fire measure.'' Goal: price the cache for real. Touches: wiki read path, prompt-cache blocks (SOURCED: 5-minute and 1-hour writes, 0.1x reads). First measure: cache hit rate, cost per question and cost per rank over two weeks of house use, set against my table. Must NOT: cache any third person's content, or ship a wiki that holds private pages into a shared prefix.
# ''Lane C, the dreaming queue.'' Goal: ask now or wake to the answer at half price. Touches: batch (SOURCED, 50% off), the receipt ledger. First measure: turnaround against the 24-hour batch window the pricing page describes (INVENTED reading: confirm the window on the batch page), plus receipt landing causal and clockless. Must NOT: promise a wall-clock dawn; hold a batch job across an epoch that retires its cap without a defined outcome.
# ''Lane D, the elder's scribe.'' Goal: transcribe an operator-chosen oral history into the wiki as a public good. Touches: `bags/` docs (MEASURED by Hearth-Reader); the model sees only material the speaker consents to. First measure: consent record, error rate on a sample read back by the speaker. Must NOT: take a third party's voice or words before W25 closes and a consent step stands; add an AI-written line to the commons without a mark naming the spirit that wrote it (answers D.3).
# ''Lane E, the incense adversary.'' Goal: a closed group of 10 to 20 trusted people, price-and-bound incense, one hired adversary farming with puppets. Touches: admission-price, veil-vouch, nullifier (MEASURED by Hearth-Reader; the vouch-ledger wiring still owes). First measure: pour gained by the adversary against pour gained by an honest newcomer, and Persona-split attempts caught per person. Must NOT before the license: convert incense into redeemable value for anyone outside the house, or bind it to coin.

