* ''Community-owned names.'' He argues for community TLDs such as dotMeow, "a way to fund good causes", over protocol-level ones, and proposes a one-click tool pairing registrars with handles. SOURCED (https://blog.bmannconsulting.com/3mx67tbnwak2y). Borrow: a TLD that funds a cause echoes the Eris tithe (take 7) and the Chiemgauer nonprofit share in Scrip-Reader's ledger (INFERRED). The house's attestation seat on TLDs may read this (INFERRED).
* ''AI, local first.'' He avoided LLM tools to keep data off US platforms, marks December 2025 as the turn when agentic coding improved, runs Z-Space Local AI (ZAI) as a local cluster for testing and bring-your-own-key tools, and bought a founders' plan from Umans, a French host of open models. SOURCED (https://bmannconsulting.com/blog/2026/06/28/bmc-next-agentic-coding/). Borrow: a pool member who prefers local or non-US inference wants the warden able to route to an open-model provider (INFERRED, ties to #/the-wave "the spend" and L4). Challenge: the house's pool buys a US provider's credits; Mann's data-sovereignty reason for abstaining is the case the pool's Gates (License, W25) must answer (INFERRED).
* ''Where value sits.'' He relays the "Barbell of Software Value": with AI-made software abundant, value gathers at platforms and at private internal systems, squeezing the generic middle. SOURCED (https://blog.bmannconsulting.com/3lxxs2xktpk2v). Borrow: the pool and Lararium sit at the platform end; avoid becoming generic SaaS (INFERRED). He also writes "Obsidian's CEO on why productivity tools need community more than AI". SOURCED (title only, https://bmannconsulting.com/blog).
* ''Networked, personalized software.'' GhostOff exports a Ghost blog to the atproto publisher Offprint; he praises "vit", a network where each person's codebase evolves by pulling capabilities from a trusted network instead of forking one repo. SOURCED (https://blog.bmannconsulting.com/3mpsppyyr2s2d). Borrow: the vit model of trusted-network capability exchange resembles a Nexus lending a cap (INFERRED); his worry about "infinite forks" meets the house's one-owner-per-registry floor (INFERRED).
* ''Personal knowledge.'' His notes run in Obsidian with wikilinks and backlinks, imported from LogSeq; he likes Lichen Wiki's backlinks and publishes through Semble and Leaflet on atproto. SOURCED (BMC Next post). A digital garden of linked notes sits close to the house's wikis; INFERRED.
* ''Futures reading.'' He gives //Rainbows End// to new collaborators as an "indoctrination file", and asks what a realistic ten-year path looks like in "jagged" adoption. SOURCED (https://blog.bmannconsulting.com/3mwdbvmhfds2r). Borrow: the house's own reading list could carry it (INFERRED).
* ''Local-first and Ink & Switch.'' His now page names Ink & Switch Lab Day and an 18-month ARIA UK grant; Ink & Switch's site confirms a 2026 Local-First Conf Lab Day (July 12 to 14, Berlin) and lists Keyhive. SOURCED (https://bmannconsulting.com/now, https://www.inkandswitch.com/). The COO role still rests on his own page: the Ink & Switch site mentions neither him nor a team page, and a second search found nothing. GAP. No Subduction or Zelenka page was found there either.
* ''ZAI and ARIA.'' ZAI runs; funding "moving toward" per the now page. SOURCED (now page). No external source on ARIA's terms. GAP.

!!! Ratchet ~ against #/mana-scrip

* ''Memberships, not balances: CONFIRMS.'' CoSocial sells a flat yearly membership with a vote, not a stored balance, and the Open Collective ledger shows only gifts and fees. SOURCED (https://opencollective.com/cosocial). That matches Scrip-Reader's design answer (a membership whose benefit renews each epoch). Real, running practice; Mann's circle shows no stored-value instrument at all.
* ''Closed loop: CONFIRMS, with a caution.'' The Community Fund and CoSocial both hold plain money in a fiscal host or registered co-op; neither issues a token. SOURCED. Nothing Mann writes treats a meter as a currency. INFERRED. The Chiemgauer's nonprofit share (Scrip-Reader's ledger) and his dotMeow-for-causes preference point the same way: tie the tithe to a cause, not a rate.
* ''Company-store warning: SHARPENS.'' Mann's move away from the US fiscal host (the Raft Foundation) toward a co-op outside the US, because the work "outgrew fiscal sponsorship", names the host-as-single-seller risk from the member's side. SOURCED (https://bmannconsulting.com/notes/atmosphere-community-co-op/). Add to Scrip-Reader's line: a member MUST be able to leave the Temple with the ledger and cap shape intact, so the sole-issuer shape never forms (INFERRED). His ZAI and Umans use also shows a lawful alternative supplier; the pool should post its per-call price and keep a second route (INFERRED).
* ''Demurrage: SILENT.'' No Mann source touches demurrage, hoarding or stamp scrip; take 5 finds no living lineage in this circle. INFERRED from null reads. The nearest practice is CoSocial's yearly renewal, which retires membership by season, not balance by season (INFERRED); that suggests the free incense and donated mana demur while a paid membership simply lapses at the epoch, answering Scrip-Reader's breakage flag without a rise-to-dish on paid value.
* ''Where he pushes against the house.'' (1) He favors open infrastructure on public-good software (LaSuite) with login by a global account; the house's pool gates a closed warden behind a license. (2) His data-sovereignty reason for avoiding US AI platforms makes the pool's single provider the exact objection a neighbor like him would raise. (3) His public books model puts minutes behind a member login; the house wants Council's amounts-by-tier rule. All INFERRED.
* ''Ask for Mann.'' Whether CoSocial's flat fee ever carried a share; how the Atmosphere Community Co-Op will charter its exit rule; whether Ink & Switch would pilot a cap-metered pool beside Keyhive.

