!! Mana-Scrip ~ hemp, flax and the seasons

//Scrip-Reader (Tasked Spirit), 2026-10-31. Every claim carries a mark: SOURCED (url) · RECALLED (from memory, unchecked) · INFERRED (the reader's step). Nothing here gives legal advice.//

!!! The Lineage ~ where flaxscrip stands

* ''The novel names it.'' //Illuminatus!// lists "Appendix Vau: Flaxscrip and Hempscrip" in its contents, and the body carries both currencies. SOURCED (text extracted from the archive.org ebook, https://archive.org/details/illuminatus-trilogy-ebook; Appendix Vau is read in Wave 2 below).
* ''Two scrips, two cabals.'' Hempscript belongs to the Norton Cabal and its circle; flaxscript to Hagbard's "Legion of Dynamic Discord". The cabals swap them and also accept the "DMM script" of the Satanist, Surrealists and Sadists: "Anything to avoid Federal Reserve notes, you know." SOURCED (same text).
* ''The Zen joke.'' Flaxscript "commemorates the Zen Master who was asked, "What is the Buddha?" and replied, "Five pounds of flax."" The classical koan reads three pounds (Wumenguan, case 18); the novel changes it to five, which suits the Law of 5s. SOURCED (novel) · RECALLED (the koan's three pounds).
* ''Instant demurrage.'' A character says of a rival bunch: "you know what they do with Federal Reserve notes. Every time they get one, they burn it. Instant demurrage, they call it." The word itself stands in the novel, so the lineage to Gesell runs explicit, not guessed. SOURCED (novel text).
* ''The anarchist banks.'' Dr. Ignotius tells the initiate that "William Green and Lysander Spooner" prized "competing currencies instead of one uniform State currency", and that "the government broke its own laws to find ways to suppress Green's Mutual Bank and Spooner's People's Bank"; Emperor Norton's money circulated because he was "so crazy that people humored him". SOURCED (novel text). The Mutual Bank belongs to William B. Greene, the novel's "William Green". RECALLED.
* ''Flaxscript stays fictional.'' The novel counts Lincoln's greenbacks as "the closest thing to flaxscript America ever had". SOURCED (novel). The appendix claims a real Discordian issue in 1968 (see Wave 2); I found no outside record. SOURCED (appendix) · INFERRED.
* ''RAW's own pen.'' His 1962 essay "Proudhon's Economics" (//Way Out//, Sept. 1962) praises the People's Bank that "lends without charging interest" and names "Gesell's stamp scrip" once, in a parenthesis. It does not mention Warren, Spooner or demurrage. SOURCED (https://theanarchistlibrary.org/library/robert-anton-wilson-proudhon-s-economics.html). I found nothing on Gesell in //Prometheus Rising// or //The Illuminati Papers//; I leave those unclaimed. INFERRED from null searches.
* ''Hemp, the other plant.'' The novel's Appendix Aleph, "George Washington's Hemp Crop", sets hemp in the Illuminati's own mythology. SOURCED (contents list, same ebook).

!!! The Scrip Ledger ~ what lived, what died

* ''Warren's Time Store (Cincinnati, 1827).'' Labor notes priced in hours; goods sold at cost plus 7 percent. Warren liquidated it around 1830 to build the village of Equity (1835), which lasted two years amid malaria. A second Equity Store ran 1842-1844. SOURCED (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Josiah_Warren). Lesson: the notes stayed a unit of account for a single shop; no one outside the shop's trust accepted them. INFERRED.
* ''Wära (Schwanenkirchen, 1929-31).'' Gesellian stamp scrip; the Reich finance ministry banned it 30 Oct 1931. SOURCED (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/W%C3%A4ra).
* ''Wörgl (1932-33).'' Mayor Unterguggenberger began issuing stamp-scrip labour certificates on 31 July 1932; the Austrian central bank moved against it, and on 18 Nov 1933 the Higher Administrative Court declared the certificates illegal, after about 200 other municipalities announced copies. SOURCED (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/W%C3%B6rgl; https://www.freitag.de/autoren/dirk-schaefer/das-wunder-von-worgl). Lesson: it died of the issuer's legal standing, not of failed circulation; a note that competes with the state's note draws the state's hand once it works.
* ''US depression scrip.'' Irving Fisher's design asked for a weekly stamp of 2 percent; some 450 US cities showed interest, yet most communities stamped per transaction and ran the scrip as relief financing. Results ran mixed. SOURCED (https://wiki.p2pfoundation.net/History_of_Stamp_Scrip). The 1933 federal move against scrip RECALLED, unchecked here.
* ''Chiemgauer (Bavaria, 2003-).'' A regional currency tied 1:1 to the euro, with demurrage (sources give 2 percent a quarter, or a daily-computed 6 percent a year in the digital form), a conversion charge back to euros, and a share of every exchange going to a local nonprofit; sources report velocity near three times that of the euro and several hundred accepting businesses. SOURCED, with conflicts between sources (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chiemgauer; https://core.ac.uk/download/12174396.pdf). Lesson: it survived by staying legal tender-adjacent: euro-backed, a consumer-side nonprofit tithe, merchants who gain customers by accepting it. INFERRED.
* ''Company scrip.'' Out of Britain's truck system (Truck Act 1831) the American coal towns took scrip honored only at the company store; by 1922 nearly 80 percent of West Virginia miners lived in company houses and shopped at company stores; advances in scrip ran 50 to 80 percent of face; a 1940 Kentucky suit began the decline, and Congress outlawed coal scrip in 1967 per Wikipedia. SOURCED (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Company_scrip) · the 1967 date carries a single secondary source.
* ''Time banks.'' Edgar Cahn's time dollars and the Warren lineage ride the same line: an hour for an hour. RECALLED; I did not check the IRS stance or the failure record.

!!! Fit ~ how mana-scrip lines up

* ''Demurrage by epoch.'' The Temple's season-turn return of unspent mana does Gesell's work without a stamp and without a clock: the Kahu act advances the epoch, so no wall time decides. INFERRED. Wörgl and the Chiemgauer both show demurrage raising velocity (SOURCED above); the pool wants the reverse of hoarding, since an idle cap blocks a neighbor's pour.
* ''The votive mark.'' A member's hold against the return gives the pool Fisher's lesson: a scheme survives when holders can route their stored value to a purpose rather than lose it flat. INFERRED.
* ''The Eris tithe.'' The blind Discord pick matches the Chiemgauer's nonprofit share and the novel's joke economy in one move. INFERRED.
* ''Closed loop beats competing currency.'' Wära and Wörgl died as rivals to the state's money. Mana never cashes out, never pays wages, never settles debts; it stays a meter on a model bridge. That removes the contest that killed the Gesellian experiments. INFERRED.
* ''The company-store warning.'' Scrip hurt miners because it was wage, redeemable at one seller, at the seller's price. The dish stays clear while mana buys only inference at a posted rate, no member's livelihood rides on it, and the Temple never pays anyone in mana. The line to hold: ''mana MUST NOT turn into wage or required payment, and its price per call MUST post publicly''; a Temple that is both sole issuer and sole seller of the only thing mana buys carries the company store's shape, so the open ledger (#/the-wave) matters twice. INFERRED.
* ''A fee that grows.'' Company scrip took its margin on conversion. The pool takes none on return; the return rises back to the dish, not to the Temple's treasury. A demurrage that feeds the issuer's income reads as the truck system in a new coat. INFERRED.

!!! Regulatory Flag ~ not legal advice

I am no lawyer; a US attorney must read this before coin touches the dish.

* ''Money transmission.'' FinCEN's prepaid-access rule exempts closed-loop access only when no more than $2,000 attaches to a device on a given day, and its 2013 virtual-currency guidance centers on //convertible// virtual currency. A closed-loop mana that never converts sits on the favorable side. SOURCED (https://www.fincen.gov/statutes_regs/guidance/pdf/FIN-2013-G001.pdf; https://perkinscoie.com/en/news-insights/fincen-issues-final-rule-regarding-prepaid-access.html); the daily cap's application to a pool balance stays untested. INFERRED. Gifting between members (#/three-feeds) and any secondary sale would blur "never cashes out"; a gift that can be re-sold for coin makes mana convertible. INFERRED.
* ''Gift-card, breakage and escheat.'' States treat unredeemed stored value differently: dormancy periods commonly run two to five years, some states exempt gift cards, others claim a share of the balance. SOURCED, dated (https://www.journalofaccountancy.com/issues/2008/dec/statesbiteintobrokengiftcards/). Demurrage that returns paid-for mana to the dish looks, to a state treasurer, like breakage. INFERRED. The federal CARD Act's five-year gift-card expiry and the treatment of loyalty points RECALLED, unchecked; here the season-turn return may collide with a minimum-life rule for paid balances. INFERRED.
* ''Design answers worth asking counsel.'' Make the tier fee buy a //membership// whose benefit renews each epoch rather than a stored balance; keep the free incense and donated mana demurring freely, and let coin-bought mana ride the votive mark or a long epoch. INFERRED.

!!! Name ~ does "mana-scrip" carry it?

''Verdict: yes, keep it; spell it flaxscrip-style.'' "Scrip" names the lineage (novel's hempscript, flaxscript, DMM script; the stamp-scrip history), and "mana" names the house's meter. The word also warns, since scrip has the company-store history behind it; a name that remembers both its parents helps keep the dish honest. The novel spells it "script" in places and the contents list says "scrip"; the house MAY use "scrip" for the instrument and keep "mana" as the unit.

Alternatives, only if you want the Discordian wink to ride stronger:
* ''Incensescrip'' ~ names the free base tended by the commons; weaker on the meter.
* ''Libation-scrip'' ~ matches the dish and the pool's title, though it hides mana.
* ''Five-pound flax'' as a nickname for the Temple's unit ~ playful, with the koan's five.

None carries the lineage better than mana-scrip for a public name; the first two work as tier or feed labels. INFERRED.

!!! Wave 2 ~ gaps closed

* ''Appendix Vau read.'' The earlier pass could not read it; my text extraction carries it (SOURCED: the archive.org ebook text, https://archive.org/details/illuminatus-trilogy-ebook, pages 444-445 of the PDF). It corrects two lines above:
** ''Not wholly fiction.'' The authors write: "Flaxscrip was first introduced into Discordian groups by the mysterious Malaclypse the Younger, K.S.C., in 1968. Hempscrip followed the year after, issued by Dr. Mordecai Malignatus, K.N.S." The novel "move[s] these coinages backward in time" and gives hempscrip to the Justified Ancients of Mummu. The Discordian origin stays a claim by two authors who joke for a living; I found no outside record of a 1968 issue. SOURCED (appendix) · INFERRED (the caution).
** ''Gesell, named.'' "Hempscrip, as employed in the novel, depreciates; it is, thus, not merely a no-interest currency, but a negative-interest currency. The lender literally pays the borrower to take it away for a while. It was invented by German business-economist Silvio Gesell, and is described in his Natural Economic Order and in professor Irving Fisher's Stamp Script." SOURCED.
* ''The appendix's own lineage.'' Dominican "Banks of Piety" lending free (via Tawney); Josiah Warren's phrase "ethical competition"; Scots free banking and colonial paper money, both suppressed (via Mullen and Pound's //Impact//); Greene's //Mutual Banking//; Warren's //True Civilization//; Spooner's //Our Financiers//; Martin's //Men Against the State// and Rocker's //Pioneers of American Freedom// for the overview. SOURCED (appendix, as the authors' own citations; I did not verify the books).
* ''RAW's own find.'' Wilson "unearthed in 1962 the tale of a no-interest currency, privately issued, in Yellow Springs, Ohio, during the 1930s depression", abandoned "as soon as the 'tight-money' squeeze ended". This fits the 1962 Proudhon essay's date. SOURCED (appendix). Lesson from its own text: a scrip tied to an emergency dies with the emergency. INFERRED.
* ''Gresham reversed.'' The appendix claims "In a libertarian society, good money will drive out the bad", and its footnote cites three small frontier communities in Warren's book plus a Master's thesis by Don Werkheiser. SOURCED (appendix; the claim itself stays unchecked and the footnote flags the skepticism). For the pool: demurring mana spends first and votive-marked mana waits, so a Gresham dynamic inside the dish helps, not harms. INFERRED.
* ''Other RAW writing on Gesell.'' A second search found nothing beyond the Proudhon essay and this appendix. The two together stand as RAW's recorded engagement; I claim nothing further. SOURCED-ABSENT.
* ''Time banks and tax.'' Secondary sources say the IRS began ruling in 1985 that time banks are not barter organizations and time dollars not taxable, on grounds that include no commission, cash cannot buy credits or clear a debt, and equal value for every hour; one source mentions a 1995 ruling without a number; a Finnish tax office in 2013 reportedly treated time credits as income. SOURCED, secondary only (https://capecodtimebank.blogspot.com/2009/06/fyi.html; https://communitycurrencieslaw.org/time-banks/); no IRS document opened, so the rulings need a primary check by counsel. Cahn's founding date and history stay RECALLED.

!!! Ratchet ~ open-co-op practice against the lessons

Read against `#/open-org-lineage` (Lineage-Reader's marks stand there; I lean on them and read none of those pages myself).

* ''Memberships, not balances ~ CONFIRMS, and SHARPENS.'' CoSocial sells a flat $50 CAD member year with an AGM vote and states "100% of our revenue comes from membership fees and contributions"; no stored balance exists to strand. The time-bank rulings add a sharper edge: their reported tests bar buying credits with cash. Mana-scrip as written lets coin buy mana, which sits on the other side of that test. So the tier fee MUST buy the membership (standing, vote, a grant of mana each epoch), never mana by the unit. INFERRED.
* ''Demurrage to the dish ~ CONFIRMS by silence, SHARPENS.'' Lineage-Reader found no demurrage anywhere in the open-co-op practice, so the seasonal return has only the Gesell and Chiemgauer line behind it (SOURCED above). The co-op's open ledger shows the check that works: let members read where the returned mana went. A return that credits the dish and posts in the ledger resembles a membership benefit; one that credits the Temple's account resembles issuer income. INFERRED.
* ''Convertibility ~ CONFIRMS.'' No source in the lineage lets members cash out a stored value; money enters as fees and leaves as spend on infrastructure. Mana mirrors that: the only exit runs through the Temple's spend, never to a member. INFERRED.
* ''Breakage ~ CHANGES the question.'' A membership fee has no unused balance to forfeit; the breakage risk arises only when coin buys a counted unit. Move the coin to the membership and the grant to the epoch, and "breakage" shrinks to unspent free grants, a gift with no paid value behind it. INFERRED; escheat treatment of such grants stays a counsel question.
* ''Fiscal host ~ CHANGES the Temple.'' Lineage-Reader reports the Community Fund outgrew its US host and Fission handed CoSocial on. The Temple that holds the bank account also becomes the sole issuer and sole seller, the company-store shape named above, and also the party a regulator reaches first. The charter needs an exit door and a posted price from day one. INFERRED.
* ''The AGM ~ BORROW.'' CoSocial's members approve the books yearly. The Aftermath season could hold the pool's statement vote, with the invariants below as the standing rules to amend. INFERRED.
* ''Mann and Zelenka ~ no bearing.'' The shared Ink & Switch employer carries no scrip lesson; the Ink & Switch line rests on one page (Lineage-Reader). Omitted.

!!! Invariants ~ for the house to rule on

Each stands as a proposal; the operator's mind originates and none binds until ruled. Numbers carry no rank.

# ''Coin buys membership, never mana by the unit.'' A tier fee grants standing and a mana grant per epoch. Tied to the time-bank tests (no cash for credits, equal valuation) and the Wörgl and Wära bans. INFERRED.
# ''Mana never converts.'' No cash-out, no resale for coin, no wage, no debt settlement; a gift between members carries no price. Tied to FinCEN's convertible-currency line and to company scrip, whose harm came as wage. INFERRED.
# ''The price per call posts publicly.'' Every rate, every pour, every return rides the open ledger by tier and season, never by face. Tied to company scrip (price set by the sole seller) and CoSocial's open books. INFERRED.
# ''Demurrage credits the dish.'' The seasonal return lands in the pool, never in Temple income; the ledger shows it. Tied to the Chiemgauer's nonprofit share and the truck system's conversion margin. INFERRED.
# ''A votive mark always holds.'' A member MAY route unspent mana to a votive before the turn. Tied to Fisher's stamp scrip, which survived where holders could route value. INFERRED.
# ''No second issuer, no sole seller.'' The Temple MUST NOT stand as the only party that issues mana and the only seller of what it buys; a second provider source exists, and a Nexus MAY graduate with its own dish. Tied to company scrip and to Lineage-Reader's host-graduation pattern. INFERRED.
# ''The epoch turns by act, never by clock.'' A Kahu act advances the season. Tied to the Wörgl stamp, which a wall-clock schedule imposed, and to the house's no-global-now law. INFERRED.
# ''The floor never closes.'' The incense base stays free and keeps a standing mana grant. Tied to Yellow Springs: scrip bound to an emergency dies when the emergency ends, so the free base holds the commons through calm seasons. INFERRED.
# ''An assembly amends the invariants.'' Members approve the books and the rules at the Aftermath vote. Tied to CoSocial's AGM. INFERRED.
# ''Counsel reads before coin flows.'' A US attorney reviews money-transmission, prepaid-access, escheat and state gift-card law before the first coin tier opens. Nothing in this ahu gives legal advice.

